A||N||5||Runner||Back Up Lights||||1||
Z||000000||Runner||04-22-2000||12:07 AM||||My 86 V6 4 speed back up lights will not stay on. They light when I shift into reverse and then quit as soon as I release the shifter.  Any ideas on cause and repair ?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fiasco||04-22-2000||02:36 AM||||I have the exact same problem. I haven't yet tested the switch, but I know it's either that or the shift lever mechanism. <BR>Looking at the shifter with the console parts off, I notice that when I push the shifter over into reverse, the little lever in there pushes back against the switch and the lights work.  But when I let go, the shifter moves to the right an inch or so and the little lever lets off the switch. <BR>Is this lever supposed to stay in contact with the switch's plunger?  Is there an adjustment?  Or is the shifter supposed to stay all the way to the left once I let go???||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jeff's '86 1/2 GT||04-22-2000||05:08 AM||||Hey, you guys are lucky.  My '86 Gt with 5spd, didn't come with the wiring from the factory for the back up lights.  And the Dealership wouldn't pay to put them in.  So for 14 years no back up lights...........||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||04-22-2000||06:16 AM||||Strange...<P>I had an 86SE that did the same thing. I ended up getting an 88 Coupe and putting the V6 from my SE in it. The SE wiring harness had a plug dangling from the speedo sensor wire and I had no clue what it was for. It turns out when I put the V6 in the 88 with the Isuzu 5 speed it plugged into the Isuzu's reverse switch and the reverse lights work!! I have looked at a few other 4 speed V6 cars and saw that they had the "extra" plug... strange.||||reg||1
Z||000004||William Federle||04-22-2000||07:18 AM||||The same thing happens in my 1984 SE. I had the console covers off and watched the action of the switch and the mechanism that activates it. It travels farther to activate the switch when you're putting it into reverse and then relaxes back away from the switch a small amount when you relax pressure on the shift lever. I tried to adjust the switch plunger out toward the thing that pushes on it. I even tried to bend the part that pushes on it with pliers, but didn't go too far with that method since I was afraid of breaking something. My switch worked for a while, but works as it had before I adjustment. I think after you get past a certain length of the switch plunger, it tends to slide off of the surface that pushes on it. I don't know what the answer is.<P>It might be that the springing back away from the switch is caused by looseness in the shifting cables - maybe adjustment of the shift mechanism would solve this problem.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 04-22-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Rodrv6||04-22-2000||10:50 AM||||My 84 has done the same thing.  The problem remained even after a new set of shift cables and after disassembling and checking the shifter--all the bushings on it were still good and snug.  I finally found the culprit!  The select cable linkage on the transaxle was worn where it pivots on the shaft. (The select cable is the one that moves when you move the shifter from side to side.) I was able to find some brass tubing and made a couple of bushings to go in the select lever.  Problem solved  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Now the backup lights work correctly and the shifter feels much more precise.  It doesn't take much wear to make a big difference.!!!||||reg||6
